  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 PM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nCurrently cyclonic flow continues across much of the Central and\nNorthern Plains around a Hudson Bay upper low. A very slight\nshortwave at the jet stream level is creating high clouds over\nthe Dakotas. This translate to the surface with a surface trough\nand light rain returns on radar. So far there are no reports of\nthis rain reaching the ground, likely due to a very dry near\nsurface atmosphere. To the west a ridge is building across the \nPacific Northwest. The rest of this afternoon near-critical fire\nweather conditions will continue across the northwest and north\ncentral as winds could gust to 30mph with the low relative\nhumidities and dry grass. Tonight a surface low pressure in\nnorthern CO could push near surface wildfire smoke into the\nstate through early Thursday morning. There is also a chance \nthat through Friday we start to have smoke aloft linger. \n\nThursday the fire weather concerns elevate enough to have a few\nhours of Red Flag conditions across the west. Therefor we have \nissued a Red Flag Warning for much of western ND for Thursday \nafternoon and evening. Along with the very dry air and breezy \nwinds, a cold front in the evening will shift winds to the \nnorthwest. With the cooler air behind the front, fire weather \nconditions will likely quickly improve as the air will be \nslightly cooler and more moist. Thursday will also be the \nwarmest day of the forecast as the ridge is at it's peak over \nthe Northern Plains. Expect widespread highs in the 90s.\n\nThe rest of this week that ridge will slowly flatten to zonal\nflow, keeping the strong thermal ridge to our south. Highs will\nthen only be in the 80s through the weekend. This zonal flow\ncould create some disturbances in the flow off the Northern\nRockies, leading to slight chances for showers and thunderstorms\nstarting Saturday night. Late this weekend the flow turns\nslightly northwesterly as the Hudson Bay low slides south \ntowards New York. This will bring some cyclonic flow into \nnorthern ND. More disturbances could form off the Canadian \nRockies leading to widespread low to medium chances for rain \nSunday night through Tuesday. Temperatures during this time look\nto stay in the 80s.",
